Flecha: flechaPrivate.navigate takes a tab to an address as the omnibox does.

The shell sent the addresses typed in its command bar and a Flechinha's
address bar through tabs.update(). Chromium makes an extension's navigation
renderer-initiated (TabsUpdateFunction::UpdateURL), and the history
manipulation intervention then marks the page left behind as skippable when
nobody clicked or typed in it: going from one site to another by its address
left Back with nowhere to go. navigate() loads the URL from the browser, with
a typed transition from the address bar, which the intervention leaves alone.
It doesn't take javascript: URLs, which would run in the page.

--- a/chrome/common/extensions/api/flecha_private.idl
+++ b/chrome/common/extensions/api/flecha_private.idl
@@ -303,6 +303,13 @@
     // Whether a tab has a page to go back to and one to go forward to, for
     // the Back and Forward buttons (tabs.goBack() and goForward() don't say).
     static void getTabHistory(long tabId, TabHistoryCallback callback);
+    // Takes a tab to an address the user typed or picked in Flecha (the
+    // command bar, a Flechinha's address), as the omnibox does: a typed,
+    // browser-initiated navigation. tabs.update() navigates as the extension
+    // would, so the page left behind is skipped by Back when nobody
+    // interacted with it. Not for javascript: URLs.
+    static void navigate(long tabId, DOMString url,
+                         optional DoneCallback callback);
